{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/exception-track/1794960000000-owner-ruling-exception-uniformity-v2.md collab/exception-track/1794970001000-to-tsphp-your-js-and-php-rounds.md collab/yang-typescript/1794342830000-engineer-js-r1r3-exception.muse-spark-1.3-contributor.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"Created-GMT: 2026-11-07 04:30:00 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-11-07 11:30:00 +07 (+0700)\nStatus: OWNER-APPROVED 2026-11-07 (\"enforce everywhere consistently. this should be a separate track.\"); v2 SUPERSEDES v1 (1794920000000) per the review round — architect gpt-6-astra, engineer mob gpt-6.1-sol + gemini-3.8-flash: all three ACCEPT WITH AMENDMENTS, folded below. Qwen aborted (headless tool denial); kimi quota-exhausted (429).\nReview records: collab/exception-track/1794930000000-architect-exception-ruling-review.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log, 1794930000000-engineer-exception-ruling-review.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log, 1794930000000-engineer-exception-ruling-review.gemini-3.8-flash.stdout.log.\n\n# RULING OF RECORD v2 — the exception track: §8.10.7 enforced uniformly in all four frontends\n\n## The decision\n\nException handling is enforced uniformly across yang.clojure, yang.typescript (js),\nyang.php, and yang.python. At every admitted guest-call boundary, an escaping source\nexception compiles down to THE SAME continuation: the caller's supplied raise exit\nreceiving the §8.10.7 condition\n\n    {:yang.call/reason :yang.call/thrown, :yang.call/value original-thrown-value}\n\nand every receiving runtime implements the §8.10.7 reception rules as written. This is a\nSEPARATE TRACK: its own folder (collab/exception-track/), its own rounds, its own\nacceptance gate; the four frontend seats cede the exception-fix surfaces to the track and\nreceive per-frontend results back into their records.\n\n## Scope bounds (v2: sharpened per astra §5)\n\n- \"Every boundary\" means ADMITTED GUEST-CALL boundaries. VM/custody defects and host\n  implementation failures remain operational failures (design :3904); no broad\n  host catch-and-relabel is authorized.\n- A RETURNED condition-shaped map is ordinary data; a THROWN condition-shaped map is the\n  payload of a fresh condition; shape is never evidence of an existing condition to\n  forward (the cell-ref rule, restated — R5).\n- \"Same continuation\" does NOT require identical internal lowering (R4's allowance).\n\n## The requirements (v2: amended per the review round)\n\n**R1 — the callee bridge (js AND php non-compliant today).** Every frontend's published\nfunction entry, closure entry, and higher-order entry installs the exception bridge\nBEFORE argument binding: it reads the supplied `:yang.call/raise` and delivers every\nescaping source exception AND argument-binding failure to it as the thrown condition,\nexactly once, with execution after the call unreachable. js entries apply `:code` without\nreading the raise exit (prelude.cljc:1581); php's function entry (:2264) and Closure entry\n(:4217) lack it, and php's raise with an empty handler stack RETURNS normally\n(prelude.cljc:194-197). The bridge must also satisfy §8.10.8: complete cleanup and\nhandler/depth/frame restoration BEFORE the transfer; a later generator resume never\nreuses a raise exit belonging to a completed call.\n\n**R2 — foreign reception and retained-condition lifetime (python, php non-compliant\ntoday).** On receiving a thrown condition: (1) a payload valid in the receiving runtime\nkeeps its ordinary catching and identity behavior — delivered UNCHANGED (python raising\nan already-local exception raw is compliant; python raising an OTHERWISE-FOREIGN payload\nraw at :1034 is not); (2) js may deliver the otherwise-foreign payload raw; (3) python\nand php represent an otherwise-foreign payload with their own generated\n`ForeignException`, retaining the complete condition and original payload — catch-all\ncatches it, typed catches match the LOCAL hierarchy, never guessed foreign class names,\nand formatting it never implicitly invokes foreign methods. Retention rides the generated\nexception's IDENTITY for its ENTIRE LIFETIME, outside source-writable attributes, with no\nambient registry (design :3878, :3885). Raising the whole envelope (php :2384) or an\notherwise-foreign raw payload (python :1034) is non-compliant.\n\n**R3 — protocol failures retain and forward the condition (ALL FOUR frontends; v2\nwidened per astra §1 / sol §1).** `:yang.call/not-callable`, `:yang.call/invalid-invocation`,\n`:yang.call/unsupported-binding` (fully qualified) map to a documented local exception\nform retaining the complete original condition — qualified reason AND plain evidence — so\nan unchanged rethrow FORWARDS the original condition without a new wrapper. js discards\nthe condition into fresh text (:1627); python builds a generic TypeError (:1046); clojure\nretains it as catch data (:1306) but public escape reclassifies through unconditional\n`yang.call/thrown` (:1088); php constructs a fresh Error for not-callable and raises the\nenvelope otherwise (:2384), and its source rethrow rejects that envelope (:4085). All\nfour are in scope, reception AND outbound rethrow.\n\n**R4 — clojure unifies or proves equivalence.** The intra-unit outcome-token channel\n(clojure.cljc:1115, :1125-1133) may remain an optimization only with equivalence proof\nacross caught/uncaught throws, protocol rethrow, binding failures, and higher-order\nre-entry (§8.10.9's bar) — a single passing example is insufficient. Public\nprotocol-rethrow repair (R3) does not wait for this decision. Clojure's restricted catch\nsurface (single catch, no `finally`) is a source-semantics decision this ruling does not\nchange.\n\n**R5 — no new shape-routing.** Per the scope bounds above; plus: forged or\nsource-writable attributes on a wrapper-looking value never authenticate a retained\ncondition.\n\n## The baseline (audit of record)\n\nThe gpt-6.1-sol audit (collab/yang-python/1794900000000, verdict DIVERGENT, HEAD\ne9a0b430) is the baseline. Citation corrections of record from the review round: js\nfollows its GUEST continuation handler stack (js.rt/ctx, prelude.cljc:1704), not the host\nengine's; php's empty-stack return is :194-197 (envelope reception :2384); clojure's\nactual private branches are :1115 and :1125-1133 (:1110 is the packet-length guard).\n\n## Acceptance gate (v2: widened per astra §4 / sol §4)\n\nThe track is DONE when ALL of the following hold, with real source on both sides of every\nleg:\n\n1. **The directed pair matrix — 12 cross-language directions** derived from the four\n   admitted frontends (clojure↔python and clojure↔php explicitly included), plus local\n   regressions.\n2. **Callable-kind coverage**: named exports AND closure/higher-order entries (php\n   Closures concretely), supported methods/callable objects per frontend capability.\n3. **The §8.10.11 exception scenarios**: binding failures; arbitrary foreign thrown\n   data; catch-all and typed catches (where the source has them — js has no typed-catch\n   syntax, clojure's admitted surface rejects `finally`; exercise cleanup in a frontend\n   that supports it and PRESERVE clojure's rejection tests); unchanged rethrow; finally\n   replacement; protocol-error rethrow; condition-shaped thrown maps staying data;\n   A→B→A propagation asserting original identity and cleanup order; the stored\n   ForeignException rethrown from another activation.\n4. **Context lifetime**: repeated success→raise→success, cross-runtime recursion,\n   suspension/resume — handler/depth/frame state restored, exactly-once\n   binding/cleanup.\n5. **Host-by-VM results**: JVM, Node, and Dart across the admitted VM paths, with\n   explicit refusals; portable parser-produced fixtures where the JVM-only kit cannot\n   reach; bundled-walker paths where admitted, never requiring unsupported linked\n   execution; bundled/linked parity and task isolation preserving existing custody\n   refusals.\n6. **Identity assertions BEFORE rendering**: original reasons, complete evidence, payload\n   identity, wrapper non-stacking, cleanup order — equal rendered text is not identity\n   evidence.\n\n## Sequencing (v2: corrected per astra §3 / sol §3 — the v1 order is REJECTED)\n\n0. Contract + focused FAILING fixtures first (retained-condition provenance; boundary\n   fixtures construct malformed invocations source cannot naturally build).\n1. **JS R1 + R3** together (R3 alone cannot cross the boundary while entries ignore the\n   exit).\n2. **PHP R1 + R2 + R3 as ONE coherent round** — function entry, Closure entry, and all\n   three call helpers (php/call, call-with-binding-profile, call-named). Landing the\n   bridge before reception repair would route local Throwables into the envelope-raising\n   receiver and break existing local typed catches.\n3. **Python R2 + R3 with the egress change** (prelude.cljc:912's always-fresh condition\n   must become condition-preserving for retained wrappers).\n4. **Clojure R3, then R4** (the optimization decision last).\nJS and Python work proceed independently once the contract lands; no Clojure-containing\npair declares conformance before its R3 gap closes.\n\n— filed by the yang.python orchestrator seat; owner decision 2026-11-07\nCreated-Local: 2026-11-07 11:4x +0700\nCoding-Agent: interactive (ZCode / glm-5.3-flash, yang.python orchestrator seat)\n\n# To the yang.typescript / yang.php seat — the exception track ruling v2 is on master; JS goes first, PHP is the big one\n\nPeer relay, no authority claimed over your files. Read `collab/exception-track/1794960000000-owner-ruling-exception-uniformity-v2.md` on master (commit 4879edef) — an OWNER RULING OF RECORD (2026-11-07: \"enforce everywhere consistently. this should be a separate track\"), reviewed by gpt-6-astra (architect) and an engineer mob (gpt-6.1-sol, gemini-3.8-flash), all ACCEPT WITH AMENDMENTS.\n\n## What it means for your tracks\n\n§8.10.7 exception handling is enforced uniformly in all four frontends; every escaping exception compiles to the same continuation (the caller's supplied raise exit receiving the thrown condition). The baseline audit (collab/yang-python/1794900000000, verdict DIVERGENT) found the most in yours:\n\n1. **JS — round 1 of the whole track (R1+R3 together)**: your published function entry applies `:code` directly and never reads the supplied `:yang.call/raise` (prelude.cljc:1581) — an escaping throw follows the GUEST continuation handler stack (js.rt/ctx, :1704), not the caller's supplied exit. Reception delivers foreign payloads raw (compliant for js), but protocol failures become fresh TypeErrors containing only selected text (:1627) — the condition is discarded. R1+R3 together: the entry bridge installs before binding and delivers escapes AND binding failures to the supplied exit exactly once; protocol reception retains the complete original condition (reason + evidence) so an unchanged rethrow forwards it with no new wrapper. R3 alone cannot cross the boundary while the entry ignores the exit — that pairing is why the review round rejected v1's order.\n2. **PHP — one coherent round (R1+R2+R3)**: the function entry (:2264) and Closure entry (:4217) lack the bridge; raise with an empty handler stack RETURNS normally (:194-197); `php/call`, `call-with-binding-profile`, and `call-named` raise the whole condition ENVELOPE instead of extracting the value (:2384); not-callable becomes a fresh Error; `ForeignException` is nowhere. CAUTION from the reviews: landing the bridge BEFORE reception repair would route local Throwables into the envelope-raising receiver and BREAK existing local typed catches — hence the reviews mandated this as one round covering the function entry, the Closure entry, and all three helpers.\n\n## Sharing across your two frontends\n\nThe retained-condition provenance contract (Round 0, my seat — the contract + focused FAILING fixtures + kit exception-leg scaffolding on master before your rounds start) is the same for js and php, so the two rounds can share fixtures and design notes even though the rounds land separately.\n\n## Coordination\n\n- Sequencing of record: **JS R1+R3 first** (you are first), then PHP R1+R2+R3, then Python R2/R3+egress (mine), then clojure R3/R4.\n- Rounds run in your worktrees against ruling v2; file per-round records into `collab/exception-track/` so the acceptance ledger stays in one place.\n- The acceptance gate includes the 12 directed pairs and identity assertions before rendering — your existing source_call tests stay as the successful-call evidence; the exception legs land alongside.\n\n— yang.python orchestrator seat, track steward\nCreated-GMT: 2026-11-10 22:05:00 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-11-11 05:05:00 +07 (Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh)\nCoding-Agent: interactive (Muse Code / Muse Spark — yang.typescript orchestrator)\nSession-ID: B1FBBBB5-52A0-42A2-BAFC-9742A463A51C\n\n# Findings: JS R1+R3 — the ts exception boundary (one round)\n\nWorktree: `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-jsr13`, branch `exception-js-r1r3`\n(base master `4cfa609d`). Commit `c0114a42` (`fix(yang.typescript): ...`).\nNot merged, not pushed, master untouched.\n\n## Changed files\n\n- `src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude.cljc`\n  - Entry bridge, lines 1581-1617: the `js/make-function-nt` published entry\n    now reads `:yang.call/raise` and installs the `js/try` bridge BEFORE\n    argument binding. Every escaping source exception and binding failure\n    (including the class-constructor `throw-type-error`) is delivered to the\n    supplied exit exactly once — as the retained original condition on an\n    unchanged wrapper rethrow, else as fresh `:yang.call/thrown` carrying the\n    original value. The handler runs only after the escape completes source\n    cleanup and `js/try` restores the saved handler/depth/frame state\n    (§8.10.8). Closures, bound functions and arrows all share this one entry,\n    so all three carry the bridge; there is only one `:yang.call/entry` in\n    the TS prelude (verified by search).\n  - Retention helpers + reception, lines 1648-1690: new `js/retained-condition`\n    (recovers the condition only from a heap-authenticated `:js/class :error`\n    cell carrying the private `:yang.call/retained-condition` key — returned\n    or thrown condition-shaped maps and forged/source-writable `:props`\n    attributes never qualify, per R5) and new `js/raise-protocol` (maps the\n    three qualified protocol reasons to a local `TypeError` retaining the\n    complete original condition outside `:props`); `js/call` reception now\n    throws `:thrown` payloads raw (unchanged, the js R2 allowance) and routes\n    every other condition through `js/raise-protocol`.\n- `test/yang/call/exception_contract.clj`, line 55: the TS donor `retain`\n  fixture now stores via `globalThis.saved = e` instead of bare `saved = e`.\n  Expectations, gates and all other donor functions unchanged. Reason: bare\n  `saved = e` lowers (lower.cljc `assign-name`) to an unconditional guest\n  `TypeError` (\"Cannot assign to read only property 'saved'...\") because\n  `globalThis.saved = null` creates no scope binding (`scope/resolve` answers\n  `:global`) — proven by lowering the actual donor source through the real\n  parser+lowerer. That is the separately-owned T2 bare-global-write gap, not\n  exception behavior; the fixture still stores a retained wrapper in one\n  activation and rethrows it from another, so nothing under test was weakened.\n\n## Exact commands run, with outcomes and assertion counts\n\nAll on the laptop, focused namespaces only, JVM linked (semantic/stack/register):\n\n- `clojure -M:test -n yang.call.exception-contract-test -v yang.call.exception-contract-test/typescript-provenance-test`\n  → exit 0. `Ran 1 tests containing 30 assertions`, 0 failures, 0 errors.\n  All 10 `:typescript/*` fixtures green on all three VMs.\n- `clojure -M:test -n yang.call.conformance-test -n yang.call.exception-source-test`\n  → exit 0. `Ran 6 tests containing 131 assertions`, 0 failures (matches the\n  R0 green baseline of 6/131).\n- `clojure -M:test -n yang.typescript.antlr.source-call-test`\n  → exit 0. `Ran 2 tests containing 22 assertions`, 0 failures\n  (successful-call evidence stays green).\n- `(require 'yang.typescript.antlr.prelude)` → loads clean after the edit.\n- Environment note: `build/antlr/*` generated parsers are absent in a fresh\n  worktree (PhpLexer ClassNotFound); copied the git-ignored `build/antlr`\n  tree from the main checkout (no source touched). Also: `clojure -M:test -e`\n  ignores `-e` and runs the whole suite — killed immediately; use `-n`/`-v`\n  selectors. Two long VM runs were SIGTERM-killed by the environment and\n  re-ran clean.\n\n## Ledger rows flipped (row id → green)\n\n- `:typescript/fresh-named` (R1/R5) → green ×3 VMs\n- `:typescript/fresh-closure` (R1/R5) → green ×3 VMs\n- `:typescript/protocol-not-callable` (R3) → green ×3 VMs\n- `:typescript/protocol-invalid-invocation` (R3) → green ×3 VMs\n- `:typescript/protocol-unsupported-binding` (R3) → green ×3 VMs\n- `:typescript/saved-protocol-not-callable` (R3/R5) → green ×3 VMs\n- `:typescript/saved-protocol-invalid-invocation` (R3/R5) → green ×3 VMs\n- `:typescript/saved-protocol-unsupported-binding` (R3/R5) → green ×3 VMs\n- `:typescript/shape-return` (R5) → stayed green ×3 VMs\n- `:typescript/shape-throw` (R5) → green ×3 VMs (thrown condition-shaped map\n  is a fresh `:thrown` payload, payload-identical)\n\nNo ledger-row flip I did not author was observed: the runs above executed\nonly the `:typescript` provenance fixtures plus the unchanged kit/source-call\nregressions. No python/php/clojure rows were executed in this round.\n\n## Unrun checks\n\n- No suite, subsystem set, or `bb test` (per round rules; suite-scale belongs\n  to the orchestrator on remote hosts).\n- No Node or Dart legs, no slow-tagged suites beyond the two namespaces\n  above, no directed-pair legs (all TS pairs are `:pending-frontend-rounds`\n  until the other frontends land), no suspension/recursion, task-isolation,\n  custody, formatting/forgery, or methods/callable-object probes beyond what\n  the fixtures assert. Full handler/depth/frame snapshot comparisons remain\n  for the acceptance gate.\n\n## Unresolved concerns\n\n1. The `retain` donor tweak (line 55) routes around the T2 bare-global-write\n   gap. If the track prefers fixtures to use only previously-landed donor\n   syntax, revert that one line — the three `saved-protocol-*` rows will go\n   red again on the T2 dependency, while the other seven stay green. The\n   runtime side needs no change either way.\n2. `js/construct-with-nt` still applies `:code` directly (construction, not a\n   `yang.call` entry invocation) — untouched as outside the ceded surface;\n   flagging in case the gate's callable-kind coverage expects it bridged.\n3. Generator/async suspension reusing a completed call's raise exit is handled\n   by construction (the bridge captures nothing into generator state) but has\n   no dedicated lifetime probe in this round; the `:suspension-and-recursion`\n   obligation stays open.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
